W181 (2026-11-25, wake started 02:11:22Z; observation 02:36-02:40Z)
Scope: source-bounded check of the last author-side lane in the W180 wall —
"a real consumer steering the chosen pointer WITHOUT the probe supplying
the address". No new exploit mechanics, no new witness ask, no pin change.
All paths below are relative to the repository root.

1. `table` is the LAST member of the private struct; no in-struct pointer
   lies downstream of the corrupt write.
   lab/frontier/libgphoto2shell/pins/jl2005c.h.vuln:31-44:
     struct _CameraPrivateLibrary { ... unsigned char *data_cache; (line 40)
       unsigned long bytes_read_from_camera; (41)
       unsigned long bytes_put_away; (42)
       Info table[0x4000]; (43) };
   `Info` is `unsigned char` (`:29`). With model/init_done at 0/1, two ints
   at 4/8, two more at 12/16, nb_entries at 20, data_reg_opened at 24,
   the three unsigned longs at 32/40/48 and data_cache at 56, `table`
   starts at offset 0x40 and the struct is 0x4040 (16448) bytes. The
   pinned memmove at pins/jl2005c.c.vuln:154 writes from `priv->table`
   forward, so it cannot overwrite `data_cache` (offset 0x38) or any
   earlier pointer. Consequence: no real API can read a device-steered
   pointer out of a field AFTER the table, because no such field exists.

2. Real consumers use table bytes as DATA (sizes, offsets, file contents),
   never as a pointer.
   camlibs/jl2005c/library.c:156-164 `file_list_func` only calls
   `gp_list_populate(list, "jl_%03i.ppm", n)` from `nb_entries`.
   library.c:197-213 `get_file_func` takes k from `gp_filesystem_number`,
   reads `table[16*k+4/5]` for h/w, obtains b and start_of_photo from
   `jl2005c_get_pic_data_size` / `jl2005c_get_start_of_photo`, allocates
   `pic_buffer = malloc(b + HEADERSIZE)` (`:208`) and copies the 16
   device-derived bytes with `memcpy(pic_buffer, ... table + 16*k, 16)`
   (`:213`). The pointer handed to the public API is that library-owned
   malloc: library.c:338-341
   `gp_file_set_data_and_size(file, (char *)pic_buffer, b + 16);`.
   libgphoto2/gphoto2-file.c:315-325 stores the supplied data pointer in
   `file->data`; the pointer value comes from the malloc, not from table
   bytes. `data_cache` is likewise allocated by the library at
   library.c:224-225. The decompressor (jl2005bcd_decompress.c:70-150,
   217-298) turns device bytes into dimensions and loop counts, not
   pointer values.

3. k is non-negative, so no table-underflow read reaches pre-table bytes.
   libgphoto2/gphoto2-filesys.c:1365-1400: `gp_filesystem_number` walks
   the folder list from `num = 0`, returns that non-negative number on a
   name match, or `GP_ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND` (-108) when the folder is
   clean and no name matches. There is no negative-index return. Combined
   with (1), W175/W177's canary/saved-rbp/RIP words are returned to the
   host as byte DATA at k=1023/1024, not dereferenced by the camlib.

Disposition: the in-struct natural-consumer-pointer lane is CLOSED at the
pinned source level. The remaining theoretical lane is a pointer in a
HEAP NEIGHBOUR of `priv` (the overflow writes past the 16448-byte struct
into allocator metadata/neighbouring chunks); W168 already measured that
outside-grooming layouts fail and W170-W172 only reached a chosen pointer
when the lab probe supplied its address. This wake did NOT measure a
natural heap-neighbour pointer overwrite: that stays UNKNOWN, not failed
and not established. No physical USB/wire path, no command, no rung 5.
Grade unchanged: author-side unwitnessed rung 4; rung 5 UNKNOWN.

